// PSS v3.0 LRM — Example 252: exec blocks added via extension (page 334)
import std_pkg::*;
action A {
  int a;
  exec pre_solve {
    a=1;
  }
  exec body {
    message(LOW,"Hello from A %d", a);
  }
}
action A1 : A {
  exec body {
    super;
    message(LOW,"Hello from A1 %d", a);
  }
}
extend action A1 {
  exec body {
   message(LOW,"Hello from A1(1) extension %d", a);
  }
}
extend action A1 {
  exec body {
   message(LOW,"Hello from A1(2) extension %d", a);
  }
}

22.2 Functions
Functions are a means to encapsulate behaviors used by actions and other entities to implement test
scenarios. Functions are called in procedural description contexts, and are akin to procedures in
conventional programming languages.
Functions can be declared in global, package, or component scopes. Functions can be static or instance
(non-static) functions. A global or package function is always static. A component function can be explicitly
declared as static. If a component function is non-static, each function call is associated with a specific
instance of that component type.
A function may be defined in one of three ways:
